Now I have the full pattern. Let me implement the Rust M2 input layer + run loop + capi. This is the biggest chunk. Let me write it carefully.

Let me design `render.rs` additions and a new `run.rs`.

Key design decisions:
- `TuiRenderer` gains: `focus: Option<u32>`, `draw_order: Vec<u32>`.
- `draw()` records draw_order (node ids in pre-order; later = drawn later = on top for hit-testing). Actually children are drawn after parents, so children are on top. draw_order in pre-order, and hit-test in reverse.
- Input mapping methods on `TuiRenderer` (they mutate focus and return Vec<Event>):
  - `pub fn on_input(&mut self, ev: &crossterm::event::Event) -> Vec<Event>`
  - handle key/mouse internally.
- Gating:
  - `desired_listeners(node)`: default POINTER_DOWN|MOVE|UP for buttons; OR EVENT_LISTENERS property. For pointer events, only send to nodes wanting them.
  - value/text events gated by BINDING_ID presence.
- Focus: focusable components = BUTTON, TOGGLE, SLIDER, STEPPER, TEXT_FIELD, TEXT_EDITOR, PICKER, MENU, DATE_PICKER. `first_focusable(root)` walks the tree.
- `node_style` draws focus with REVERSED modifier on the focused node (pass focus id).

Let me write the input handling in detail:

```rust
use ratatui::crossterm::event::{Event as TermEvent, KeyCode, KeyEvent, KeyEventKind, KeyModifiers, MouseButton, MouseEvent, MouseEventKind};
use pathland_core::{Event, listener, component_type, property_id};

const FOCUSABLE: [u16; N] = [BUTTON, TOGGLE, SLIDER, STEPPER, TEXT_FIELD, TEXT_EDITOR, PICKER, MENU, DATE_PICKER];
```

Pointer coordinates: crossterm mouse gives `(column, row)` 1-based; ratatui coords are 0-based. `col - 1`, `row - 1`.

`hit_test(col, row)`:
```rust
fn hit_test(&self, col: u16, row: u16) -> Option<u32> {
    // draw_order is pre-order; reverse to get top-most (last drawn).
    for id in self.draw_order.iter().rev() {
        if let Some(r) = self.rects.get(id) {
            if r.contains(Position { x: col, y: row }) { return Some(*id); }
        }
    }
    None
}
```
Rect::contains takes ratatui::layout::Position.

`on_input`:
```rust
pub fn on_input(&mut self, ev: &TermEvent) -> Vec<Event> {
    match ev {
        TermEvent::Mouse(m) => self.on_mouse(m),
        TermEvent::Key(k) => self.on_key(k),
        _ => vec![],
    }
}
```

`on_mouse`:
```rust
fn on_mouse(&mut self, m: &MouseEvent) -> Vec<Event> {
    let col = m.column.saturating_sub(1);
    let row = m.row.saturating_sub(1);
    match m.kind {
        MouseEventKind::Down(MouseButton::Left) => {
            let target = self.hit_test(col, row);
            self.mouse_down = target;
            if let Some(id) = target { self.focus = Some(id); }
            self.pointer_event(id, DOWN, col, row, false)
        }
        MouseEventKind::Up(MouseButton::Left) => {
            let id = self.mouse_down.or_else(|| self.hit_test(col,row));  // for click
            self.mouse_down = None;
            self.pointer_event(id, UP, col, row, false)
        }
        MouseEventKind::Moved | MouseEventKind::Drag(_) => self.pointer_event(self.hit_test(col,row), MOVE, col, row, true),
        MouseEventKind::ScrollUp | ScrollDown => wheel event to hit target,
        _ => vec![],
    }
}
```
pointer_event gating: only send if the node exists and wants the listener bit. For buttons default listeners.

```rust
fn pointer_event(&self, id: Option<u32>, command: u8, col, row, secondary) -> Vec<Event> {
    let Some(id) = id else { return vec![] };
    if !self.node_wants(id, bit_for(command)) { return vec![]; }
    let ev = match command {
        DOWN => Event::PointerDown { target: id, x: col as f32, y: row as f32, secondary },
        MOVE => Event::PointerMove { target: id, x: col as f32, y: row as f32, hovering: true, leaving: false },
        UP => Event::PointerUp { target: id, x: col as f32, y: row as f32, secondary },
    };
    vec![ev]
}
```
`node_wants(id, bit)`:
```rust
fn node_wants(&self, id: u32, bit: u32) -> bool {
    let Some(node) = self.tree.node(id) else { return false };
    let mask = node.u32_property(property_id::EVENT_LISTENERS, 0) | desired_default(node);
    mask & bit != 0
}
fn desired_default(node) -> u32 { if node.component_type == BUTTON { listener::POINTER } else { 0 } }
```
listener::POINTER = DOWN|MOVE|UP (bit 0-2). Let me check pathland_core::listener constants: POINTER_DOWN=1<<0, POINTER_MOVE=1<<1, POINTER_UP=1<<2, POINTER = DOWN|MOVE|UP. Good.

Wheel:
```rust
MouseEventKind::ScrollUp => wheel(target, 0, -1),
MouseEventKind::ScrollDown => wheel(target, 0, 1),
```
wheel event: Event::Wheel { target, delta_x, delta_y }, gated by WHEEL bit (1<<9). Actually the GTK uses EventControllerScroll for WHEEL and adjustments for SCROLL. For mouse wheel in TUI, send WHEEL (bit 9). 

`on_key`:
```rust
fn on_key(&mut self, k: &KeyEvent) -> Vec<Event> {
    if k.kind != KeyEventKind::Press { return vec![]; }
    // Focus-based navigation
    match k.code {
        KeyCode::Tab => { self.advance_focus(); return vec![]; }
        KeyCode::BackTab => { self.retreat_focus(); return vec![]; }
        _ => {}
    }
    let Some(fid) = self.focus else { return vec![] };
    let Some(node) = self.tree.node(fid) else { self.focus = None; return vec![] };
    match node.component_type {
        TEXT_FIELD | TEXT_EDITOR => self.on_text_key(fid, &node, k),
        BUTTON => match k.code {
            Enter | Char(' ') => self.activate(fid),
            _ => self.key_event(fid, k),
        },
        TOGGLE => match k.code {
            Enter | Char(' ') => self.toggle_value(fid, &node),
            _ => self.key_event(fid, k),
        },
        SLIDER => match k.code {
            Left => self.adjust_value(fid, &node, -step),
            Right => self.adjust_value(fid, &node, step),
            Down => adjust -step, Up => adjust +step,
            _ => self.key_event(fid, k),
        },
        STEPPER => similar +,- or Up/Down,
        _ => self.key_event(fid, k),
    }
}
```

`key_event(fid, k)`: gated by KEY_DOWN bit (1<<3) → Event::KeyDown { target, key_code, modifiers, repeat }. key_code mapping: for printable chars use the char's codepoint; special keys use constants. Since the app mainly uses pointer/tap + value/text events, KEY_DOWN gating is rarely on. I'll map: Char(c) → c as u16 (codepoint); else a small table (Enter=13, Esc=27, Tab=9, Backspace=8, Left=0x1B0... hmm). Let me define a simple mapping: 
- Char(c) → c as u32 & 0xFFFF
- Enter → 0x0A? Actually let me just map a few: Enter=13, Esc=27, Tab=9, Backspace=8, Delete=127, Left=0x8001... This is renderer-defined; the app doesn't use it much. Keep minimal.

`on_text_key`: if a text field is focused:
