The test:
```java
@Test
void effectRetriesAfterThrowingInsteadOfDying() {
    // A transient error must not permanently sever the effect from its signal:
    // producers are restored on the failed run, so the next change re-runs it.
    WritableSignal<Integer> a = Signals.signal(0);
    AtomicInteger runs = new AtomicInteger();
    AtomicInteger errors = new AtomicInteger();
    EffectRef effect = Signals.effect(() -> {
        runs.incrementAndGet();
        int v = a.get();
        if (v == 1) {
            errors.incrementAndGet();
            throw new IllegalStateException("transient");
        }
    });
    assertEquals(1, runs.get(), "initial run succeeds (a=0)");

    a.set(1); // the effect throws
    assertEquals(2, runs.get(), "the effect ran and threw");
    assertEquals(1, errors.get());

    a.set(2); // the effect must retry (producers restored)
    assertEquals(3, runs.get(), "a throwing effect is not permanently unsubscribed");
    effect.destroy();
}
```

Wait — `Signals.effect(() -> { runs++; ... throw ... })` — the initial run (a=0) doesn't throw. Then a.set(1) → effect re-runs → throws → the EffectException propagates from set(). So `a.set(1)` will THROW EffectException. I need to catch it (assertThrows). Let me wrap:
```java
assertThrows(EffectException.class, () -> a.set(1));
```
Then a.set(2) → effect re-runs (producers restored) → runs=3, no throw.

Let me verify the flow with my Effect change:
- Effect created → schedule → flush → runIfScheduled: dirty=true, firstRun=true → push → previous = [] (empty, first run) → resetProducers → fn.run() → runs=1, a.get() (a=0) → records a as producer → returns. error=null.
- a.set(1) → propagate → effect dirty + schedule → flush → runIfScheduled: dirty=true, refreshProducers (a is not computed) → dirty=false → firstRun=false → depsChanged (a.version changed 0→1) → true → push → previous = [a] → resetProducers (removes from a.consumers) → fn.run() → runs=2, a.get()=1 → throws → catch: error=t → restoreProducers(previous=[a]) → re-add a.consumers += effect → dirty=false → throw EffectException → propagates through set().
- a.set(2) → propagate → effect in a.consumers → markDirtyAndPropagate → dirty=true → schedule → flush → runIfScheduled: dirty=true → refreshProducers → dirty=false → firstRun=false → depsChanged (a.version 1→2) → true → runs (producers=[a]) → fn.run() → runs=3, a.get()=2 → no throw. 

So the test passes with the fix. Without the fix (previous behavior), after the throw at a.set(1), producers=[] → a.set(2) → effect NOT in a.consumers → not re-scheduled → runs stays 2 → the assertion `runs==3` fails. Good — the test proves the fix.

